An order desk must distinguish text that is not a number from a number that violates a business rule. Parsing answers the first question; a comparison answers the second. `int.Parse` assumes valid text and can throw. `int.TryParse` reports ordinary conversion failure as a Boolean so a decision or loop can respond without exception handling.

General call pattern (schematic):

```csharp
int quantity;
bool converted = int.TryParse(text, out quantity);
```

`text` is the input string. `out quantity` identifies a variable the library method fills. You are calling an existing method, not defining one. It is safe to read `quantity` afterward, but use it as an accepted order quantity only when conversion succeeds and the range check passes. On conversion failure this overload writes zero, which does not mean the customer entered zero. Chapter 5 explains how to define your own output parameters.

| Input | Conversion | Range 1 through 20 | Response |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `"many"` | False | Not evaluated as an order | Ask again. |
| `"0"` | True | False | Ask again. |
| `"12"` | True | True | Accept 12. |
| Missing input (`null`) | False | Not accepted | End input rather than retry forever. |

Read the trace across each row: conversion is necessary but insufficient. The loop below checks conversion before range and stops on end of input. Type `many`, then `0`, then `12` to follow the first three rows. It prints two rejection messages and accepts 12. This interactive listing is a VS Code application; its result depends on the supplied input.

```csharp
using System;
int quantity = 0;
while (true)
{
    Console.Write("Quantity (1-20): ");
    string? text = Console.ReadLine();
    if (text == null) break;
    if (int.TryParse(text, out quantity) && quantity >= 1 && quantity <= 20)
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"Accepted {quantity}");
        break;
    }
    Console.WriteLine("Enter a whole number from 1 through 20.");
}
```

A common mistake is to call `Parse` first and try to validate afterward: invalid text fails before the comparison runs. Another is retrying forever after `ReadLine` returns null. A sentinel such as `quit` is a third policy: test it before attempting conversion.

See the language/API reference for this contract.[^gap-validation]

